<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aschaffenburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aschaffenburg is a city on the Main in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-franconia">Lower Franconia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>. For centuries it was the second residence of the archbishop-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, who built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannisburg-palace">Johannisburg Palace</a> there in 1605–1614. After 1803 it was the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Karl Theodor von Dalberg</a>'s state, and it passed to Bavaria in 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg.jpg" alt="Aschaffenburg" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rainer Lippert →; derivative work by Steffen Schmitz (Carschten) · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aschaffenburger_Schloss,_edit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The final settlement came with the Treaty of Munich of 1816: Bavaria, which had already given back Tyrol and Vorarlberg and gained Würzburg and <b>Aschaffenburg</b> in 1814, returned Salzburg and the Innviertel to Austria and received the Palatinate on the left bank of the Rhine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His state was scattered and Catholic, with <b>Aschaffenburg</b> as its richest part.</blockquote><a href="/state/archchancellors-electorate">Archchancellor's Electorate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Reichsdeputationshauptschluss of 1803 dissolved the electorate; its last elector, Karl Theodor von Dalberg, kept a state centred on <b>Aschaffenburg</b> and Regensburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-mainz">Electorate of Mainz</a></li><li><blockquote>These were combined with Frankfurt, <b>Aschaffenburg</b> and Wetzlar to form the Grand Duchy of Frankfurt, with Napoleon's stepson Eugène de Beauharnais named as Dalberg's successor.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-frankfurt">Grand Duchy of Frankfurt</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Aschaffenburg</b> had been the second residence of the archbishops of Mainz for centuries, with the large Renaissance Johannisburg palace.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-aschaffenburg">Principality of Aschaffenburg</a></li></ul></section>
